{
  "schema_version": 2,
  "name": "sed — stream editor",
  "description": "Line-oriented text editor. Reads input one line at a time, applies a script of substitution / deletion / insertion / print commands to each line, and writes the result. Great for find-and-replace across files, deleting matching lines, reformatting line by line, and other one-off rewriting tasks.\n\nNew to sed? Click the 'Help & reference' tab for a crash course (anatomy, addresses, common one-liners). Source: https://www.gnu.org/software/sed/manual/",
  "executable": "sed",
  "working_directory": null,
  "sections": [
    { "name": "Script", "layout": "tab" },
    { "name": "Mode", "layout": "tab" },
    { "name": "Help & reference", "layout": "tab" }
  ],
  "argument_template": [
    "{suppress_print?-n}",
    "{extended_regex?-E}",
    "{in_place?-i}",
    "{nul_separated?-z}",
    "{separate_streams?-s}",
    ["-l", "{line_wrap}"],
    ["-f", "{script_file}"],
    ["-e", "{script}"],
    "{input_files}"
  ],
  "params": [
    {
      "id": "script",
      "label": "Script (-e)",
      "description": "The sed script — one or more sed commands separated by semicolons or newlines.\n\nExamples to copy:\n  Find/replace first match per line:    s/old/new/\n  Find/replace ALL matches per line:    s/old/new/g\n  Delete lines matching regex:          /pattern/d\n  Print only matching lines (with -n):  /pattern/p\n  Strip Windows line endings:           s/\\r$//\n  Two commands, semicolon-separated:    s/foo/bar/g; s/baz/qux/g\n\nDon't surround the whole script in quotes — ScripTree passes it as a single argv token already.\n\nLeave blank if you're loading the script from 'Script file' instead.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "textarea",
      "required": false,
      "default": "",
      "section": "Script"
    },
    {
      "id": "script_file",
      "label": "Script file (-f)",
      "description": "Read the script from this file instead of typing it inline. Useful for longer multi-command scripts you reuse. If you fill BOTH this and 'Script' inline, sed runs both (concatenated). Picking one is usually clearer.",
      "type": "path",
      "widget": "file_open",
      "required": false,
      "default": "",
      "file_filter": "sed scripts (*.sed);;All files (*)",
      "section": "Script"
    },
    {
      "id": "input_files",
      "label": "Input files",
      "description": "Files to process. Space-separated for multiple. Leave blank to read from stdin.\n\nBy default sed treats all input files as one big stream — addresses like '1' or '$' apply to the whole concatenation. Tick 'Separate streams' below to make each file independent.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "text",
      "required": false,
      "default": "",
      "section": "Script"
    },
    {
      "id": "suppress_print",
      "label": "Suppress auto-print (-n / --quiet)",
      "description": "By default sed prints every line after running the script. -n turns that off — sed only outputs lines you explicitly print with the 'p' command. Essential for grep-style behaviour: `-n '/error/p'` prints only lines matching 'error'.\n\nLeave off for substitute-and-print workflows; tick on for filter / extract workflows.",
      "type": "bool",
      "widget": "checkbox",
      "required": false,
      "default": false,
      "section": "Mode"
    },
    {
      "id": "extended_regex",
      "label": "Extended regex (-E / -r)",
      "description": "Use POSIX extended regular expressions instead of basic ones. Without this, you have to backslash-escape +, ?, (, ), and | to make them special. With it, you escape them to make them LITERAL — same convention as grep -E and most modern regex engines.\n\nLeave off for traditional sed scripts; tick on for cleaner-looking modern regex.",
      "type": "bool",
      "widget": "checkbox",
      "required": false,
      "default": false,
      "section": "Mode"
    },
    {
      "id": "in_place",
      "label": "In-place edit (-i)",
      "description": "OVERWRITE the input files with the result, instead of printing to stdout. Useful for batch find-and-replace across a file tree.\n\n⚠ DESTRUCTIVE — there's no undo. Test the script first WITHOUT -i (it'll print the would-be output) before running with -i.\n\nNote: BSD sed (macOS default) requires `-i ''` (an empty backup-suffix arg). GNU sed accepts bare `-i`. The form emits bare `-i` — works on Linux / Git Bash gawk-sed / WSL. On stock macOS, run `brew install gnu-sed` and adjust 'executable' to gsed for cross-platform consistency.",
      "type": "bool",
      "widget": "checkbox",
      "required": false,
      "default": false,
      "section": "Mode"
    },
    {
      "id": "separate_streams",
      "label": "Treat files separately (-s / --separate)",
      "description": "(GNU sed only) Process each input file as its own independent stream rather than concatenating them. Affects line numbers (each file restarts from 1) and addresses like '$' (each file's last line, not just the overall last). Off = old default behaviour.",
      "type": "bool",
      "widget": "checkbox",
      "required": false,
      "default": false,
      "section": "Mode"
    },
    {
      "id": "nul_separated",
      "label": "NUL-separated lines (-z / --null-data)",
      "description": "(GNU sed only) Treat input as NUL-separated rather than newline-separated. Pair with `find -print0` or `xargs -0` when filenames contain newlines or other troublesome characters.",
      "type": "bool",
      "widget": "checkbox",
      "required": false,
      "default": false,
      "section": "Mode"
    },
    {
      "id": "line_wrap",
      "label": "Wrap output at column (-l)",
      "description": "(GNU sed only) Specifies the line-wrap length used by the 'l' (list) command — when sed prints lines unambiguously with control characters escaped, this is where it wraps. Default 70. Rarely needs changing.",
      "type": "integer",
      "widget": "number",
      "required": false,
      "default": 0,
      "min": 0,
      "max": 999,
      "section": "Mode"
    },
    {
      "id": "_help_anatomy",
      "label": "Anatomy of a sed script",
      "description": "Reference only — this field has no effect on what's run. Read it, copy snippets out of it, ignore it.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "textarea",
      "required": false,
      "default": "A sed script is a list of commands. Each command has the shape:\n\n  [address] command [arguments]\n\nThe most useful commands:\n\n  s/regex/replacement/[flags]   substitute matching text\n  d                             delete the line (don't print it)\n  p                             print the line — use with -n to print only matches\n  N                             append the next line to pattern space (for multi-line work)\n  i\\text                        insert 'text' as a new line BEFORE the current line\n  a\\text                        append 'text' as a new line AFTER the current line\n  c\\text                        replace the current line with 'text'\n  q                             quit immediately\n  =                             print the current line number\n  y/abc/xyz/                    transliterate (single-char swap, like tr)\n\nMultiple commands can be separated by semicolons or newlines:\n\n  s/foo/bar/g; s/baz/qux/g\n\nFor the 's' (substitute) command, the most common flags are:\n\n  g            global — replace ALL matches on the line, not just the first\n  i            (GNU) case-insensitive matching\n  p            print the line if a substitution was made\n  N            replace only the Nth match (e.g. s/foo/bar/2 replaces the 2nd 'foo')",
      "section": "Help & reference"
    },
    {
      "id": "_help_addresses",
      "label": "Addresses (limit a command to certain lines)",
      "description": "Reference only — this field has no effect on what's run.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "textarea",
      "required": false,
      "default": "By default a command applies to every line. Prefix it with an 'address' to limit it.\n\n  5command              line 5 only\n  $command              the last line\n  1,5command            lines 1-5 (range)\n  1,$command            line 1 to the end\n  /regex/command        every line matching regex\n  /start/,/end/command  from a 'start' line through an 'end' line (inclusive)\n  0~3command            (GNU) every 3rd line starting from line 0\n  /regex/!command       (negation) every line NOT matching regex\n\nExamples:\n\n  5d                    delete line 5\n  1,10d                 delete lines 1-10\n  $d                    delete the last line\n  /^DEBUG/d             delete lines starting with 'DEBUG'\n  /BEGIN/,/END/d        delete everything from a BEGIN line through an END line\n  /^$/!p                (with -n) print only non-blank lines",
      "section": "Help & reference"
    },
    {
      "id": "_help_oneliners",
      "label": "Common one-liners (copy/paste into 'Script')",
      "description": "Reference only — this field has no effect on what's run. Copy any of these into the Script tab.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "textarea",
      "required": false,
      "default": "SUBSTITUTE:\n  s/foo/bar/                    replace first 'foo' on each line\n  s/foo/bar/g                   replace ALL 'foo' on each line\n  s/foo/bar/gi                  global, case-insensitive (GNU)\n  s|/path/old|/path/new|g       use | as delimiter when '/' is in the pattern\n  s/^/  /                       indent every line with 2 spaces\n  s/[[:space:]]*$//             strip trailing whitespace\n  s/^[[:space:]]*//             strip leading whitespace\n  s/\\r$//                       strip Windows line endings\n\nDELETE:\n  /pattern/d                    delete lines matching pattern\n  /^$/d                         delete blank lines\n  /^#/d                         delete comment lines\n  5d                            delete line 5\n  1,10d                         delete first 10 lines\n  $d                            delete the last line\n\nPRINT (use with -n / 'Suppress auto-print'):\n  /pattern/p                    print matching lines (grep clone)\n  5p                            print line 5\n  1,10p                         print first 10 lines\n  $p                            print the last line (tail -1 clone)\n  /BEGIN/,/END/p                print everything between two markers\n\nMULTI-COMMAND (semicolon-separated):\n  s/foo/bar/g; s/baz/qux/g      two substitutes in one pass\n  /^$/d; s/\\r$//                strip blank lines AND Windows line endings\n\nIN-PLACE (DESTRUCTIVE — test first!):\n  -i 's/foo/bar/g' file.txt     overwrite file.txt\n\nSee 'man sed' or https://www.gnu.org/software/sed/manual/ for the full reference.",
      "section": "Help & reference"
    },
    {
      "id": "_help_form",
      "label": "About this form",
      "description": "Reference only — this field has no effect on what's run.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "textarea",
      "required": false,
      "default": "How the form maps to sed's CLI:\n\n  Script tab\n    Script           → -e SCRIPT (the inline script). Don't wrap it in quotes —\n                       ScripTree passes it as a single argv token already.\n    Script file      → -f FILE. Use INSTEAD of inline, or alongside (sed will\n                       run both, concatenated).\n    Input files      → positional file arguments. Space-separated. Leave blank\n                       to read from stdin.\n\n  Mode tab\n    Suppress auto-print (-n)   sed only prints lines you explicitly 'p' — essential\n                                for grep-style filtering. Off = sed prints every line.\n    Extended regex (-E)        +, ?, (, ), | are special without escaping. Standard\n                                modern regex feel.\n    In-place edit (-i)         OVERWRITE the input files instead of printing to\n                                stdout. DESTRUCTIVE; test first.\n    Treat files separately(-s) (GNU) each file is its own stream — line numbers\n                                and '$' addresses reset per file.\n    NUL-separated (-z)         (GNU) input is split by NUL bytes, not newlines.\n                                Use with find -print0 / xargs -0.\n    Wrap output at column (-l) (GNU) line-wrap length for the 'l' (list) command.\n\nIN-PLACE GOTCHA: GNU sed accepts bare '-i', BSD sed (macOS) requires '-i \"\"' (an\nexplicit empty backup-suffix). The form emits bare -i, which works on Linux,\nGit Bash, WSL, and macOS via 'brew install gnu-sed' (then change the form's\nexecutable to 'gsed').",
      "section": "Help & reference"
    }
  ],
  "source": {
    "mode": "manual",
    "url": "https://www.gnu.org/software/sed/manual/",
    "help_text_cached": "sed [OPTION]... {script-only-if-no-other-script} [input-file]...\n\nA stream editor. Edits an input stream by applying a script of\ncommands line-by-line.\n\n  -n, --quiet, --silent      suppress automatic printing\n  -e SCRIPT                  add the script to the commands to be executed\n  -f SCRIPT-FILE             add contents of script-file to the commands\n  -E, -r, --regexp-extended  use extended regular expressions\n  -i[SUFFIX]                 edit files in place (optional backup suffix)\n  -s, --separate             consider files as separate streams\n  -z, --null-data            separate lines by NUL characters\n  -l, --line-length=N        line-wrap length for the 'l' command\n\nMan: man sed\nGNU sed manual: https://www.gnu.org/software/sed/manual/"
  }
}
